Subtask 1.4: Verify Application Deployment
Check that all services and pods are running correctly:

# Check all pods are running
oc get pods -n bookinfo-microservices

# Check all services are created
oc get services -n bookinfo-microservices

# Verify sidecar injection occurred
oc get pods -n bookinfo-microservices -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.containers[*].name}{"\n"}{end}'
You should see that each pod has two containers: the application container and the istio-proxy sidecar container.

Task 2: Ensure Application Services Communicate via Service Mesh
Subtask 2.1: Create Gateway and VirtualService
To enable external access and configure traffic routing through the service mesh, we need to create Istio Gateway and VirtualService resources.

# Create Gateway for external access
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: bookinfo-gateway
  namespace: bookinfo-microservices
spec:
  selector:
    istio: ingressgateway
  servers:
  - port:
      number: 80
      name: http
      protocol: HTTP
    hosts:
    - "*"
EOF
# Create VirtualService for traffic routing
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: bookinfo
  namespace: bookinfo-microservices
spec:
  hosts:
  - "*"
  gateways:
  - bookinfo-gateway
  http:
  - match:
    - uri:
        exact: /productpage
    - uri:
        prefix: /static
    - uri:
        exact: /login
    - uri:
        exact: /logout
    - uri:
        prefix: /api/v1/products
    route:
    - destination:
        host: productpage
        port:
          number: 9080
EOF
Subtask 2.2: Configure DestinationRules
Create DestinationRules to define service versions and load balancing policies:

# Create DestinationRules for all services
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: productpage
  namespace: bookinfo-microservices
spec:
  host: productpage
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: reviews
  namespace: bookinfo-microservices
spec:
  host: reviews
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
  - name: v2
    labels:
      version: v2
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: ratings
  namespace: bookinfo-microservices
spec:
  host: ratings
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: details
  namespace: bookinfo-microservices
spec:
  host: details
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Test Service-to-Service Communication
Verify that services can communicate through the service mesh:

# Get the ingress gateway URL
export INGRESS_HOST=$(oc get route istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "Ingress Host: $INGRESS_HOST"

# Test the application
curl -s "http://$INGRESS_HOST/productpage" | grep -o "<title>.*</title>"
Subtask 2.4: Verify Internal Service Communication
Test internal service communication by executing commands inside a pod:

# Get a productpage pod name
PRODUCTPAGE_POD=$(oc get pods -n bookinfo-microservices -l app=productpage -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')

# Test communication to details service
oc exec -n bookinfo-microservices $PRODUCTPAGE_POD -c productpage -- curl -s details:9080/details/0

# Test communication to reviews service
oc exec -n bookinfo-microservices $PRODUCTPAGE_POD -c productpage -- curl -s reviews:9080/reviews/0
Task 3: Verify Traffic Routing Through Proxies
Subtask 3.1: Examine Sidecar Proxy Configuration
Check the Envoy proxy configuration to understand how traffic is being routed:

# Get detailed information about a pod's sidecar
PRODUCTPAGE_POD=$(oc get pods -n bookinfo-microservices -l app=productpage -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')

# Check Envoy admin interface
oc exec -n bookinfo-microservices $PRODUCTPAGE_POD -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/help
Subtask 3.2: Monitor Traffic with Proxy Logs
Enable and examine proxy access logs to verify traffic routing:

# Check current proxy logs
oc logs -n bookinfo-microservices $PRODUCTPAGE_POD -c istio-proxy --tail=10

# Generate some traffic
for i in {1..5}; do
  curl -s "http://$INGRESS_HOST/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Request $i sent"
  sleep 1
done

# Check proxy logs again to see the traffic
oc logs -n bookinfo-microservices $PRODUCTPAGE_POD -c istio-proxy --tail=20
Subtask 3.3: Implement Traffic Splitting
Create a VirtualService to split traffic between different versions of the reviews service:

# Create traffic splitting rule
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews
  namespace: bookinfo-microservices
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
      weight: 50
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
      weight: 50
EOF
Subtask 3.4: Verify Traffic Splitting
Test the traffic splitting by making multiple requests and observing different responses:

# Make multiple requests to see traffic splitting
for i in {1..10}; do
  echo "Request $i:"
  curl -s "http://$INGRESS_HOST/productpage" | grep -A 5 -B 5 "reviews"
  echo "---"
  sleep 2
done
Subtask 3.5: Monitor Service Mesh Metrics
Check service mesh metrics to verify proxy involvement:

# Check proxy statistics
oc exec -n bookinfo-microservices $PRODUCTPAGE_POD -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/stats | grep reviews

# Check cluster information
oc exec -n bookinfo-microservices $PRODUCTPAGE_POD -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/clusters | grep reviews
Advanced Verification and Monitoring
Subtask 3.6: Use Kiali for Traffic Visualization
Access Kiali to visualize service mesh traffic:

# Get Kiali route
oc get route kiali -n istio-system

# Generate continuous traffic for visualization
while true; do
  curl -s "http://$INGRESS_HOST/productpage" > /dev/null
  sleep 1
done
